{"id":"gen-1790978346-lNN6h3JUkXW08X4DR4QV","object":"chat.completion","created":1790978346,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c1-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c1-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c1-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c1\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0,\n            3,\n            1\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"a\",\n          \"b\",\n          \"c\",\n          \"d\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c1-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c4-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c1-proposer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve a CSP with variables a through p (16 variables). The constraints are pairwise constraints between variables, each specifying allowed pairs (value1, value2) from domain {0,1,2,3}. There are also \"bridges\" constraints that link variables across components: c17 between b (c1) and e (c2) etc. The problem asks: \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c1, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nSo we need to find all possible assignments to variables a,b,c,d (the component c1) that can be extended to a full solution for the entire CSP (i.e., there exists assignments for e..p satisfying all constraints). We must enumerate all such assignments for c1 that appear in any full solution.\n\nWe need to produce a JSON envelope matching the template, with the \"tuples\" field containing all satisfying assignments for c1 (each assignment is a list of four ints in order a,b,c,d). The tuples should be sorted lexicographically (i.e., by a then b then c then d). We must ensure we output exactly the JSON envelope with no extra text, no markdown.\n\nWe must compute the set of possible (a,b,c,d) values.\n\nThe CSP appears to be a constraint satisfaction problem with a cycle of constraints across components: c1 variables a,b,c,d; c2 variables e,f,g,h; c3 i,j,k,l; c4 m,n,o,p. There are constraints c1..c16 (pairwise constraints) connecting adjacent variables in a chain: c1 is a-b, c2 b-c, c3 c-d, c4 d-a (closing the loop within c1). So within c1, we have a 4-cycle constraints.\n\nThen c5..c8 are within c2: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e (forming a 4-cycle within c2). Then c9..c12 are within c3: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i (forming a 4-cycle within c3). Then c13..c16 are within c4: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m (forming a 4-cycle within c4). So each component is a 4-cycle.\n\nThen there are \"bridges\" constraints linking across components: c17 connects b (c1) and e (c2). c18 connects f (c2) and i (c3). c19 connects j (c3) and m (c4). c20 connects n (c4) and a (c1). So we have a \"global cycle\" linking components: b-e (c1-c2), f-i (c2-c3), j-m (c3-c4), n-a (c4-c1). This yields a global cycle linking all components.\n\nThus the overall CSP is a 16-variable cyclic CSP with constraints forming a single loop: a-b (c1), b-e (bridge), e-f (c2), f-i (bridge), i-j (c3), j-m (bridge), m-n (c4), n-a (bridge), plus internal constraints for each component.\n\nThus we need to find all assignments for a,b,c,d that can be extended.\n\nWe can brute-force search theoretically. Domain size is 4 for each variable, total possibilities 4^16 = 4.29 billion, too many to brute force manually, but we can use reasoning or systematic enumeration. However, we can possibly solve analytically by constraint propagation. But maybe we can reason about the structure: each component is a 4-cycle with its own allowed pairs constraints. The bridges also impose constraints. The problem likely has a manageable number of solutions.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible assignments for a,b,c,d. Since each variable domain is 0-3, we could try to enumerate all possibilities for a,b,c,d (4^4 = 256) and for each check if there exists extension for the remaining variables. That is doable manually with a program, but here we need to reason manually. But we can attempt to systematically explore using logical deduction.\n\nWe can treat this as a CSP with constraints that are small. Possibly there are not many solutions overall, maybe a few. So enumerating all a,b,c,d combos that appear in any solution may be manageable.\n\nAlternatively, we can attempt to solve the entire CSP using reasoning, find all solutions, then extract the c1 assignments.\n\nWe need to produce the JSON envelope with all those assignments. So we need to compute the set.\n\nGiven the complexity, we need to systematically explore. Let's attempt to solve using constraint reasoning manually, but we can also simulate mentally using deduction, but that might be tedious. However, we can try to reduce the search space using constraints.\n\nFirst, list all constraints for each component.\n\nComponent c1 constraints (c1..c4):\n- c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [ [1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3] ].\nSo allowed (a,b):\n(1,0)\n(1,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,3)\n(0,1)\n(0,3)\n\n- c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: [ [0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2] ].\nSo allowed (b,c):\n(0,3)\n(1,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,2)\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n\n- c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: [ [3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1] ].\nSo allowed (c,d):\n(3,1)\n(0,1)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\n- c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: [ [1,1], [2,1], [1,2], [2,2], [2,0], [3,2] ].\nSo allowed (d,a):\n(1,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,2)\n(2,2)\n(2,0)\n(3,2)\n\nThus c1 component constraints form a cycle.\n\nNow component c2 constraints (c5..c8):\n- c5: (e,f) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0] ].\nSo allowed (e,f):\n(2,2)\n(1,2)\n(3,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,0)\n(2,0)\n\n- c6: (f,g) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3] ].\nSo allowed (f,g):\n(2,2)\n(1,1)\n(0,0)\n(1,3)\n(0,2)\n(0,3)\n\n- c7: (g,h) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1] ].\nSo allowed (g,h):\n(2,0)\n(3,2)\n(1,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,3)\n(3,1)\n\n- c8: (h,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ].\nSo allowed (h,e):\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(3,0)\n(2,1)\n\nThus c2 forms a cycle.\n\nComponent c3 constraints (c9..c12):\n- c9: (i,j) allowed: [ [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3] ].\nSo allowed (i,j):\n(0,2)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n(3,2)\n(2,1)\n(2,3)\n\n- c10: (j,k) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0] ].\nSo allowed (j,k):\n(2,3)\n(0,0)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n(3,0)\n\n- c11: (k,l) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3] ].\nSo allowed (k,l):\n(3,0)\n(0,3)\n(1,2)\n(1,0)\n(3,1)\n(3,3)\n\n- c12: (l,i) allowed: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ].\nSo allowed (l,i):\n(0,0)\n(3,3)\n(2,1)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n(1,3)\n\nComponent c4 constraints (c13..c16):\n- c13: (m,n) allowed: [ [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2] ].\nSo allowed (m,n):\n(3,2)\n(2,2)\n(3,3)\n(1,1)\n(0,2)\n(1,2)\n\n- c14: (n,o) allowed: [ [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2] ].\nSo allowed (n,o):\n(2,3)\n(3,2)\n(1,0)\n(1,2)\n(0,3)\n(2,2)\n\n- c15: (o,p) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ].\nSo allowed (o,p):\n(3,3)\n(1,0)\n(0,0)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(3,2)\n\n- c16: (p,m) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0] ].\nSo allowed (p,m):\n(3,3)\n(1,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,1)\n(0,1)\n(3,0)\n\nBridge constraints:\n- c17: (b,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [0,3] ].\nThus (b,e) must be either (0,2) or (0,3). So b must be 0, e is 2 or 3.\n\n- c18: (f,i) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,1] ].\nThus (f,i) must be either (2,0) or (3,1). So f is 2 or 3, i is 0 or 1 accordingly.\n\n- c19: (j,m) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0] ].\nThus (j,m) must be either (2,3) or (0,0). So j is 2, m is 3, or j=0,m=0.\n\n- c20: (n,a) allowed: [ [2,1], [3,3] ].\nThus (n,a) must be either (2,1) or (3,3). So n=2,a=1 or n=3,a=3.\n\nThus we have four bridge constraints linking components.\n\nGoal: find all assignments for a,b,c,d that can be extended.\n\nWe need to consider all possible values for a,b,c,d satisfying c1-c4 constraints, and also the bridge constraints linking them to other variables.\n\nWe also need to consider the rest of the variables (e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p) must satisfy their component constraints and bridge constraints.\n\nThus it's a global CSP.\n\nWe could try to solve systematically using constraint propagation.\n\nApproach: treat the whole system as a cycle of components. Since each component is a 4-cycle with constraints, we could possibly compute possible values for each variable via forward/backward checking.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to reduce the problem: The bridge constraints restrict b, f, j, n and their counterparts e,i,m,a. So we can treat them as fixed relationships.\n\nWe can enumerate possible assignments for the bridge variables:\n\n- Bridge c17: b=0, e=2 or 3. So b is forced to 0. That simplifies c1 constraints because a,b must be allowed pair from c1. Let's check c1 allowed pairs: (a,b) allowed pairs list includes (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3). For b=0, we need pairs where second component is 0. Let's see which allowed pairs have b=0: (1,0) is allowed. Also (???) any other? (??) The list: [ [1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3] ]. So only (1,0) has b=0. So a must be 1. So a=1. Good.\n\nThus from bridge c17 we deduce b=0, a=1, e=2 or 3.\n\nThus a is fixed to 1. Good.\n\nNow c20: (n,a) allowed: (2,1) or (3,3). Since a=1, the only allowed is (2,1). So n=2, a=1. So n=2. Good.\n\nThus we have a=1, b=0, n=2.\n\nNow c19: (j,m) allowed: (2,3) or (0,0). So j,m can be either (2,3) or (0,0). So two possibilities.\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: (2,0) or (3,1). So f,i either (2,0) or (3,1). Two possibilities.\n\nc17 already used.\n\nNow we have a and b fixed. Next, constraints involving a and b in component c1: we already used c1 (a,b) constraint satisfied (a=1,b=0). Next, c2 (b,c) constraint: b=0, so we need allowed (0,c) pairs from c2 allowed pairs list: allowed (b,c) pairs: [ [0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2] ]. So for b=0, c must be 3 or 2 (since (0,3) and (0,2) are allowed). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus c is either 2 or 3.\n\nNext, c3 (c,d) constraint: allowed (c,d) pairs: [ [3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1] ]. So for each possible c value we can see allowed d.\n\nIf c=2: allowed (c,d) pairs with c=2? None of the listed pairs have first element 2. So c=2 is not allowed in any (c,d) pair. So c cannot be 2. So c must be 3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3 (c=3,d) allowed pairs: from list, (3,1) is allowed. Also any other with c=3? Only (3,1). So d=1.\n\nThus d=1.\n\nNow we have a=1,b=0,c=3,d=1. So c1 assignment is fully determined: (1,0,3,1).\n\nNow we need to check that this assignment satisfies all c1 constraints: c1 (a,b) = (1,0) allowed, yes. c2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed? Yes, (0,3) is in c2 allowed pairs. c3 (c,d) = (3,1) allowed, yes. c4 (d,a) = (1,1) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (1,1). Yes. So c1 assignment is valid.\n\nThus there is exactly one possible assignment for a,b,c,d given the bridge constraints? Wait, we deduced b=0 from bridge c17. That forced a=1. Then c forced to 3, d forced to 1. So indeed only one assignment for c1 satisfies all constraints given the bridge constraints.\n\nBut we need to verify that there are no other possibilities for b from bridge c17? Bridge c17 allowed (b,e) pairs: [ [0,2], [0,3] ]. So b must be 0 indeed. So no other b.\n\nThus a is forced to 1 via c1. So c1 assignment is uniquely determined.\n\nThus the answer is a single tuple: [1,0,3,1].\n\nBut we must ensure that there exists a full solution for the rest of the variables (e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p) consistent with all constraints. Since we deduced a,b,c,d uniquely, we must check that there exists at least one extension for the rest. If not, then c1 assignment would be invalid (no full solution). But we need to check that there is at least one solution for the rest given these values.\n\nThus we need to see if we can assign e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe have fixed:\n- a=1\n- b=0\n- c=3\n- d=1\n- e ∈ {2,3} (from c17)\n- n=2 (from c20)\n- j,m ∈ {(2,3),(0,0)} (c19)\n- f,i ∈ {(2,0),(3,1)} (c18)\n- Also constraints for other variables.\n\nWe also have constraints in component c2: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e. So we need to assign e,f,g,h consistent with those constraints and bridge constraints linking e to b (already satisfied) and f to i.\n\nSimilarly c3: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i; with j,m and f,i constraints.\n\nc4: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m; with n fixed to 2, m from c19, etc.\n\nThus we need to find at least one solution for the rest.\n\nLet's attempt to find assignments.\n\nWe have variables:\n\n- e: either 2 or 3.\n- f: either 2 or 3 (depending on which branch of c18)\n- i: accordingly 0 or 1.\n- g, h: unknown.\n- j,m: either (2,3) or (0,0).\n- k,l: unknown.\n- o,p: unknown.\n\nAlso constraints:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: [ [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0] ].\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3] ].\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1] ].\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ].\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: [ [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3] ].\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0] ].\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3] ].\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ].\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed: [ [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2] ].\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed: [ [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2] ].\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ].\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0] ].\n\nAlso bridge constraints:\nc17: (b,e) = (0,2) or (0,3). Since b=0, e must be 2 or 3. So e is either 2 or 3 (consistent with earlier). So e=2 or 3.\n\nc18: (f,i) = (2,0) or (3,1). So f,i either (2,0) or (3,1).\n\nc19: (j,m) = (2,3) or (0,0). So j,m either (2,3) or (0,0).\n\nc20: (n,a) = (2,1) or (3,3). Since a=1, n=2 (as we have). So c20 satisfied.\n\nThus we have a consistent partial assignment.\n\nNow we need to see if we can find assignments for the rest.\n\nWe need to consider the two possibilities for e (2 or 3). For each, we need to assign f,i accordingly (two possibilities). And j,m accordingly (two possibilities). So total 2*2*2 = 8 combos. For each we need to see if we can assign g,h,k,l,o,p to satisfy constraints.\n\nWe need to check constraints for each component.\n\nLet's start enumerating possibilities.\n\nWe'll denote branch selections:\n\n- e ∈ {2,3}\n- (f,i) branch: either (2,0) or (3,1). Let's denote branch A: f=2,i=0; branch B: f=3,i=1.\n- (j,m) branch: either (2,3) or (0,0). Let's denote branch C: j=2,m=3; branch D: j=0,m=0.\n\nThus 8 combos.\n\nNow we need to see for each combination if there exist assignments for g,h,k,l,o,p satisfying constraints.\n\nWe need to check constraints for component c2 (e,f,g,h) and c3 (i,j,k,l) and c4 (m,n,o,p). Let's treat each component separately.\n\nComponent c2 constraints: (e,f) allowed pairs from c5, (f,g) from c6, (g,h) from c7, (h,e) from c8.\n\nGiven e and f, we need to find g,h satisfying the cycle.\n\nSimilarly c3: given i,j, find k,l satisfying constraints.\n\nc4: given m,n (n=2), find o,p satisfying constraints.\n\nThus we need to see for each branch combination if these subproblems have solutions.\n\nLet's enumerate all possibilities.\n\nWe'll treat e=2 or 3.\n\nCase enumeration:\n\n1. e=2, f=2, i=0 (branch A for f,i), j=2,m=3 (branch C), j=2,m=3.\n2. e=2, f=2, i=0, j=0,m=0 (branch D).\n3. e=2, f=3, i=1, j=2,m=3.\n4. e=2, f=3, i=1, j=0,m=0.\n\n5. e=3, f=2, i=0, j=2,m=3.\n6. e=3, f=2, i=0, j=0,m=0.\n7. e=3, f=3, i=1, j=2,m=3.\n8. e=3, f=3, i=1, j=0,m=0.\n\nNow for each, we need to check c2 constraints (e,f,g,h). Let's list allowed pairs for each relevant constraint.\n\nc5 (e,f) allowed pairs: [ [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0] ].\n\nThus allowed (e,f) pairs:\n- (2,2) allowed.\n- (2,0) allowed.\n- (3,1) allowed.\n- (1,2) allowed.\n- (1,3) allowed.\n- (1,0) allowed.\n\nThus for each combination (e,f) we need to check if it's allowed.\n\nc6 (f,g) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (f,g):\n- (2,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n\nc7 (g,h) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1] ].\n\nThus allowed (g,h):\n- (2,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,1)\n\nc8 (h,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ].\n\nThus allowed (h,e):\n- (0,2)\n- (1,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,1)\n\nThus we need to find g,h such that (e,f) allowed, (f,g) allowed, (g,h) allowed, (h,e) allowed.\n\nSimilarly for c3 constraints.\n\nc9 (i,j) allowed: [ [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (i,j):\n- (0,2)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (2,3)\n\nc10 (j,k) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0] ].\n\nThus allowed (j,k):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,1)\n- (2,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,0)\n\nc11 (k,l) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (k,l):\n- (3,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (3,3)\n\nc12 (l,i) allowed: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (l,i):\n- (0,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (2,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,3)\n\nc4 constraints: c13 (m,n) allowed: [ [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2] ].\n\nGiven n=2, allowed (m,2):\n- (3,2)\n- (2,2)\n- (3,3) but second is 3, not 2, so not allowed.\n- (1,1) second 1, not allowed.\n- (0,2)\n- (1,2)\n\nThus allowed m values with n=2: m can be 3,2,0,1 (but need to match pairs exactly). So possible (m,n) pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (0,2), (1,2). So m ∈ {0,1,2,3} but only those four combos.\n\nc14 (n,o) allowed: [ [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2] ].\n\nGiven n=2, allowed (2,o):\n- (2,3)\n- (3,2) not (2,3) but (3,2) is (n=3) not applicable.\n- (1,0) not (2,?) no.\n- (1,2) not (2,?) no.\n- (0,3) not (2,?) no.\n- (2,2)\n\nThus allowed (n=2,o) pairs: (2,3) and (2,2). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc15 (o,p) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ].\n\nThus allowed (o,p) pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2).\n\nc16 (p,m) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0] ].\n\nThus allowed (p,m) pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0).\n\nThus we need to find o,p,m satisfying c13-c16 given m from c13 and n=2.\n\nNow we need to check each of the 8 combos for existence of solutions for c2, c3, c4.\n\nWe can try to solve each case manually.\n\nFirst, we can also consider constraints linking c2 and c3 via f,i and i,j etc. But they are separate except for f,i and i,j and j,m linking.\n\nThus we can treat each component subproblem independent given the bridge assignments.\n\nThus for each combination, we need to check if there exists g,h satisfying c2 constraints, and k,l satisfying c3 constraints, and o,p,m satisfying c4 constraints.\n\nWe also need to check any cross constraints: c2 has (f,g) and (g,h) and (h,e). c3 has (i,j) and (j,k) and (k,l) and (l,i). c4 has (m,n) and (n,o) and (o,p) and (p,m). The only cross constraints are those already accounted: f,i (c18) and j,m (c19) and b,e (c17) and n,a (c20). So after fixing e,f,i,j,m, we have independent subproblems.\n\nThus we can solve each component separately.\n\nThus the problem reduces to checking existence of solutions for each component given the fixed values.\n\nThus we need to enumerate possibilities for each case.\n\nLet's start with case 1: e=2, f=2, i=0, j=2, m=3.\n\nCase 1: e=2, f=2, i=0, j=2, m=3.\n\nCheck c2 constraints:\n\n- (e,f) = (2,2). Is (2,2) allowed in c5? Yes, (2,2) is listed.\n\n- (f,g) = (2,g) must be allowed in c6. Allowed (f,g) pairs: (2,2) is allowed. Also (2,g) where g=2? That's the only with f=2. There is no (2,0) or (2,1) etc. So g must be 2.\n\nThus g=2.\n\n- (g,h) = (2,h) must be allowed in c7. Allowed (g,h) pairs: (2,0) and (2,1) are allowed. (2,2) is not listed. So h can be 0 or 1.\n\n- (h,e) = (h,2) must be allowed in c8. Allowed (h,e) pairs: (0,2), (2,3), (2,1)?? Wait c8 allowed pairs: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ]. So allowed (h,e) = (h,2) means h=0 with e=2 is allowed (0,2). Also (h=2) with e=2? (2,2) is not listed. (h=1) with e=2? (1,2) not listed. (h=3) with e=2? (3,2) not listed. So only h=0 works.\n\nThus h must be 0.\n\nNow check (g,h) = (2,0) is allowed in c7? Yes, (2,0) is listed. So consistent.\n\nThus for case 1, we have g=2, h=0.\n\nThus c2 solved: (e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0). All constraints satisfied.\n\nNow c3 constraints: i=0, j=2.\n\n- (i,j) = (0,2) must be allowed in c9. Allowed (i,j) pairs: (0,2) is listed. Good.\n\n- (j,k) = (2,k) must be allowed in c10. Allowed (j,k) pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (2,? also (2,?)? The list includes (2,3), (2,2). Also (2,? maybe (2,? )? No other with j=2. So k can be 3 or 2.\n\n- (k,l) = (k,l) must be allowed in c11. We'll consider each possible k.\n\n- (l,i) = (l,0) must be allowed in c12. Allowed (l,i) pairs: (0,0), (2,0), (??). Let's list c12 allowed pairs: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ]. So allowed (l,i) = (l,0) includes (0,0) and (2,0). So l can be 0 or 2.\n\nThus we need to find k,l such that (j,k) allowed, (k,l) allowed, and (l,i) allowed.\n\nWe have i=0, j=2.\n\nWe need to consider possible k values: 2 or 3.\n\nCase k=2:\n- (j,k) = (2,2) allowed? Yes, (2,2) is in c10 allowed pairs.\n- (k,l) = (2,l) must be allowed in c11. Allowed (k,l) pairs: (2,l) where l=?? Let's list c11 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3). No (2,l) pairs. So (2,l) not allowed for any l. Thus k cannot be 2. So k must be 3.\n\nCase k=3:\n- (j,k) = (2,3) allowed? Yes, (2,3) is in c10 allowed pairs.\n- (k,l) = (3,l) must be allowed in c11. Allowed (3,l) pairs: (3,0), (3,1), (3,3). So l can be 0,1,3.\n\n- (l,i) = (l,0) must be allowed in c12. Allowed (l,0) pairs: (0,0) and (2,0). So l must be 0 or 2. But from above, l ∈ {0,1,3}. Intersection gives l=0 only. So l=0.\n\nThus we have k=3, l=0.\n\nCheck (k,l) = (3,0) is allowed in c11? Yes, (3,0) is listed.\n\nCheck (l,i) = (0,0) allowed in c12? Yes, (0,0) is listed.\n\nThus c3 solved: i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0.\n\nNow c4 constraints: m=3, n=2.\n\n- (m,n) = (3,2) must be allowed in c13. Allowed (m,n) pairs: (3,2) is listed. Good.\n\n- (n,o) = (2,o) must be allowed in c14. Allowed (2,o) pairs: (2,3) and (2,2). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- (o,p) = (o,p) must be allowed in c15. Allowed (o,p) pairs: for o=2, allowed pairs? Let's list c15 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for o=2, there is no pair with first element 2. So o cannot be 2. For o=3, allowed pairs: (3,3) and (3,2). So p can be 3 or 2.\n\nThus o must be 3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow (o,p) = (3,p) must be allowed: p ∈ {3,2}.\n\n- (p,m) = (p,3) must be allowed in c16. Allowed (p,m) pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0). So (p,3) allowed only if p=3? Let's see: (3,3) is allowed. (p=0) with m=3? (0,3) is not listed. (p=1) with m=3? (1,3) not listed. (p=2) with m=3? (2,3) not listed. (p=3) with m=3? (3,3) allowed. So p must be 3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nNow check (p,m) = (3,3) allowed in c16? Yes.\n\nThus c4 solved: m=3,n=2,o=3,p=3.\n\nThus case 1 yields a full solution: all variables assigned.\n\nThus c1 assignment (1,0,3,1) is part of a solution.\n\nThus at least one solution exists.\n\nNow we need to enumerate all possible c1 assignments. We found only one possible assignment for a,b,c,d given the bridge constraints. However, we need to verify that there are no alternative possibilities for b? Bridge c17 forced b=0. So c1 assignment is forced. So there is exactly one tuple for c1.\n\nBut we must double-check that there are no alternative solutions for c1 that satisfy all constraints but maybe with different b? Bridge c17 allowed only (0,2) and (0,3). So b must be 0. So indeed b=0. So c1 assignment is forced.\n\nThus the answer should be a single tuple [1,0,3,1].\n\nBut we need to be careful: Are there any other possibilities for a,b,c,d that also satisfy all constraints but perhaps with different values for a,b,c,d that still satisfy all constraints but not necessarily consistent with the bridge constraints? The bridge constraints are part of the constraints. So any solution must satisfy them. So b must be 0, a must be 1, c=3, d=1. So indeed only one assignment.\n\nThus the answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to double-check if there could be any alternative solutions where the bridge constraints are satisfied but maybe with different values for a,b,c,d? Let's examine if any other assignment for a,b,c,d could satisfy c1 constraints and also satisfy bridge constraints if we consider alternative possibilities for b? Bridge c17 only allows b=0. So b is forced to 0. So any solution must have b=0. Then c1 constraint (a,b) allowed pairs: only (1,0) is allowed. So a=1. So a forced.\n\nNow c2 (b,c) allowed pairs: (0,c) allowed if c=3 or 2. So c ∈ {2,3}. But c3 (c,d) allowed pairs: only (3,1) for c=3, none for c=2. So c must be 3, d=1. So indeed forced.\n\nThus c1 assignment is unique.\n\nThus answer is a single tuple.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON envelope with that tuple.\n\nThe envelope template is given. We need to edit only payload.claim.value.tuples. The initial template had a placeholder tuple [[0,0,0,0]]. We need to replace with our tuple(s). The envelope must match the template exactly except for that field.\n\nThus we need to output:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c1-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c1-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c1-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c1\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0,\n            3,\n            1\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"a\",\n          \"b\",\n          \"c\",\n          \"d\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c1-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c4-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c1-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure that the JSON is exactly as required. No extra whitespace? Typically whitespace is okay, but we should preserve formatting similar to template. The template uses indentation with 2 spaces? It appears to have indentation. The output should be raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }. It didn't say to preserve indentation exactly, but we can keep similar formatting.\n\nWe need to ensure that the \"tuples\" field contains exactly one tuple: [ [1,0,3,1] ].\n\nWe need to ensure that the \"variables\" list matches exactly: [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"].\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope with the tuple.\n\nWe should double-check that there are no other possible solutions for c1 that could be derived if we consider alternative assignments for the bridge constraints? Bridge constraints are fixed as above. So indeed only one.\n\nThus answer is the JSON envelope with that tuple.\n\nBefore finalizing, we should double-check that there is no hidden nuance: The problem statement says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c1, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to output all assignments for c1 that appear in any satisfying assignment of the whole CSP. We found exactly one. So we output that.\n\nBut we should double-check that there is no alternative solution where b=0, a=1, c=3, d=1 but maybe other values for a,b,c,d? No.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nBut we need to double-check that we haven't missed any alternative assignment for c1 that could be consistent with the bridge constraints if we consider alternative assignments for e that affect c1? Bridge c17 only involves b and e. So b forced 0, e can be 2 or 3. But c1 constraints do not involve e. So c1 unaffected by e value. So c1 assignment is independent of e's value. So indeed only one.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we must output the JSON envelope exactly as required.\n\nWe need to ensure that we don't include any extra keys or fields beyond those in the template. The template includes \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\", \"schema_version\", \"sender\". The \"payload\" includes \"claim\". The \"claim\" includes \"claim_id\", \"dependencies\", \"provenance\", \"revision\", \"state_revision\", \"status\", \"value\". The \"value\" includes \"component\", \"tuples\", \"variables\". So we need to preserve all fields.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope.\n\nOne more thing: The \"dependencies\" array includes an object with \"claim_revision\": 1 and \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\". That's fine.\n\nThe \"read_refs\" includes two entries: one for problem:csp-6412 and one for claim:c1-tuples.\n\nThus we need to keep those exactly.\n\nThus final answer: JSON with tuple [1,0,3,1].\n\nWe need to ensure that we output raw unformatted JSON. The template uses indentation with 2 spaces. We can output with indentation for readability, but it's still valid JSON. The instruction says \"Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\" It does not forbid pretty printing. It just says raw unformatted JSON. Usually that means no extra text, but whitespace is fine. However, \"unformatted\" might mean no extra spaces? But typical JSON can be pretty printed. I'd produce a compact representation? But it's okay to have indentation.\n\nBetter to output exactly as the template but with the tuple replaced. The template uses indentation of 2 spaces. So we can keep that.\n\nThus final answer.\n\nBut before final, we should double-check that there are no other possible assignments for c1 that could be derived if we consider alternative assignments for b from bridge c17? Bridge c17 allowed (b,e) = (0,2) or (0,3). So b must be 0. So no alternative.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nThus we will output the JSON envelope.\n\nOne more check: The \"tuples\" field is an array of arrays. The template had \"[[0,0,0,0]]\". We replace with \"[[1,0,3,1]]\". Ensure proper commas.\n\nThus final answer.\n\nNow produce final JSON.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve a CSP with variables a through p (16 variables). The constraints are pairwise constraints between variables, each specifying allowed pairs (value1, value2) from domain {0,1,2,3}. There are also \"bridges\" constraints that link variables across components: c17 between b (c1) and e (c2) etc. The problem asks: \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c1, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nSo we need to find all possible assignments to variables a,b,c,d (the component c1) that can be extended to a full solution for the entire CSP (i.e., there exists assignments for e..p satisfying all constraints). We must enumerate all such assignments for c1 that appear in any full solution.\n\nWe need to produce a JSON envelope matching the template, with the \"tuples\" field containing all satisfying assignments for c1 (each assignment is a list of four ints in order a,b,c,d). The tuples should be sorted lexicographically (i.e., by a then b then c then d). We must ensure we output exactly the JSON envelope with no extra text, no markdown.\n\nWe must compute the set of possible (a,b,c,d) values.\n\nThe CSP appears to be a constraint satisfaction problem with a cycle of constraints across components: c1 variables a,b,c,d; c2 variables e,f,g,h; c3 i,j,k,l; c4 m,n,o,p. There are constraints c1..c16 (pairwise constraints) connecting adjacent variables in a chain: c1 is a-b, c2 b-c, c3 c-d, c4 d-a (closing the loop within c1). So within c1, we have a 4-cycle constraints.\n\nThen c5..c8 are within c2: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e (forming a 4-cycle within c2). Then c9..c12 are within c3: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i (forming a 4-cycle within c3). Then c13..c16 are within c4: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m (forming a 4-cycle within c4). So each component is a 4-cycle.\n\nThen there are \"bridges\" constraints linking across components: c17 connects b (c1) and e (c2). c18 connects f (c2) and i (c3). c19 connects j (c3) and m (c4). c20 connects n (c4) and a (c1). So we have a \"global cycle\" linking components: b-e (c1-c2), f-i (c2-c3), j-m (c3-c4), n-a (c4-c1). This yields a global cycle linking all components.\n\nThus the overall CSP is a 16-variable cyclic CSP with constraints forming a single loop: a-b (c1), b-e (bridge), e-f (c2), f-i (bridge), i-j (c3), j-m (bridge), m-n (c4), n-a (bridge), plus internal constraints for each component.\n\nThus we need to find all assignments for a,b,c,d that can be extended.\n\nWe can brute-force search theoretically. Domain size is 4 for each variable, total possibilities 4^16 = 4.29 billion, too many to brute force manually, but we can use reasoning or systematic enumeration. However, we can possibly solve analytically by constraint propagation. But maybe we can reason about the structure: each component is a 4-cycle with its own allowed pairs constraints. The bridges also impose constraints. The problem likely has a manageable number of solutions.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible assignments for a,b,c,d. Since each variable domain is 0-3, we could try to enumerate all possibilities for a,b,c,d (4^4 = 256) and for each check if there exists extension for the remaining variables. That is doable manually with a program, but here we need to reason manually. But we can attempt to systematically explore using logical deduction.\n\nWe can treat this as a CSP with constraints that are small. Possibly there are not many solutions overall, maybe a few. So enumerating all a,b,c,d combos that appear in any solution may be manageable.\n\nAlternatively, we can attempt to solve the entire CSP using reasoning, find all solutions, then extract the c1 assignments.\n\nWe need to produce the JSON envelope with all those assignments. So we need to compute the set.\n\nGiven the complexity, we need to systematically explore. Let's attempt to solve using constraint reasoning manually, but we can also simulate mentally using deduction, but that might be tedious. However, we can try to reduce the search space using constraints.\n\nFirst, list all constraints for each component.\n\nComponent c1 constraints (c1..c4):\n- c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [ [1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3] ].\nSo allowed (a,b):\n(1,0)\n(1,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,3)\n(0,1)\n(0,3)\n\n- c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: [ [0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2] ].\nSo allowed (b,c):\n(0,3)\n(1,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,2)\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n\n- c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: [ [3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1] ].\nSo allowed (c,d):\n(3,1)\n(0,1)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\n- c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: [ [1,1], [2,1], [1,2], [2,2], [2,0], [3,2] ].\nSo allowed (d,a):\n(1,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,2)\n(2,2)\n(2,0)\n(3,2)\n\nThus c1 component constraints form a cycle.\n\nNow component c2 constraints (c5..c8):\n- c5: (e,f) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0] ].\nSo allowed (e,f):\n(2,2)\n(1,2)\n(3,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,0)\n(2,0)\n\n- c6: (f,g) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3] ].\nSo allowed (f,g):\n(2,2)\n(1,1)\n(0,0)\n(1,3)\n(0,2)\n(0,3)\n\n- c7: (g,h) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1] ].\nSo allowed (g,h):\n(2,0)\n(3,2)\n(1,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,3)\n(3,1)\n\n- c8: (h,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ].\nSo allowed (h,e):\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(3,0)\n(2,1)\n\nThus c2 forms a cycle.\n\nComponent c3 constraints (c9..c12):\n- c9: (i,j) allowed: [ [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3] ].\nSo allowed (i,j):\n(0,2)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n(3,2)\n(2,1)\n(2,3)\n\n- c10: (j,k) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0] ].\nSo allowed (j,k):\n(2,3)\n(0,0)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n(3,0)\n\n- c11: (k,l) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3] ].\nSo allowed (k,l):\n(3,0)\n(0,3)\n(1,2)\n(1,0)\n(3,1)\n(3,3)\n\n- c12: (l,i) allowed: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ].\nSo allowed (l,i):\n(0,0)\n(3,3)\n(2,1)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n(1,3)\n\nComponent c4 constraints (c13..c16):\n- c13: (m,n) allowed: [ [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2] ].\nSo allowed (m,n):\n(3,2)\n(2,2)\n(3,3)\n(1,1)\n(0,2)\n(1,2)\n\n- c14: (n,o) allowed: [ [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2] ].\nSo allowed (n,o):\n(2,3)\n(3,2)\n(1,0)\n(1,2)\n(0,3)\n(2,2)\n\n- c15: (o,p) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ].\nSo allowed (o,p):\n(3,3)\n(1,0)\n(0,0)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(3,2)\n\n- c16: (p,m) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0] ].\nSo allowed (p,m):\n(3,3)\n(1,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,1)\n(0,1)\n(3,0)\n\nBridge constraints:\n- c17: (b,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [0,3] ].\nThus (b,e) must be either (0,2) or (0,3). So b must be 0, e is 2 or 3.\n\n- c18: (f,i) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,1] ].\nThus (f,i) must be either (2,0) or (3,1). So f is 2 or 3, i is 0 or 1 accordingly.\n\n- c19: (j,m) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0] ].\nThus (j,m) must be either (2,3) or (0,0). So j is 2, m is 3, or j=0,m=0.\n\n- c20: (n,a) allowed: [ [2,1], [3,3] ].\nThus (n,a) must be either (2,1) or (3,3). So n=2,a=1 or n=3,a=3.\n\nThus we have four bridge constraints linking components.\n\nGoal: find all assignments for a,b,c,d that can be extended.\n\nWe need to consider all possible values for a,b,c,d satisfying c1-c4 constraints, and also the bridge constraints linking them to other variables.\n\nWe also need to consider the rest of the variables (e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p) must satisfy their component constraints and bridge constraints.\n\nThus it's a global CSP.\n\nWe could try to solve systematically using constraint propagation.\n\nApproach: treat the whole system as a cycle of components. Since each component is a 4-cycle with constraints, we could possibly compute possible values for each variable via forward/backward checking.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to reduce the problem: The bridge constraints restrict b, f, j, n and their counterparts e,i,m,a. So we can treat them as fixed relationships.\n\nWe can enumerate possible assignments for the bridge variables:\n\n- Bridge c17: b=0, e=2 or 3. So b is forced to 0. That simplifies c1 constraints because a,b must be allowed pair from c1. Let's check c1 allowed pairs: (a,b) allowed pairs list includes (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3). For b=0, we need pairs where second component is 0. Let's see which allowed pairs have b=0: (1,0) is allowed. Also (???) any other? (??) The list: [ [1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3] ]. So only (1,0) has b=0. So a must be 1. So a=1. Good.\n\nThus from bridge c17 we deduce b=0, a=1, e=2 or 3.\n\nThus a is fixed to 1. Good.\n\nNow c20: (n,a) allowed: (2,1) or (3,3). Since a=1, the only allowed is (2,1). So n=2, a=1. So n=2. Good.\n\nThus we have a=1, b=0, n=2.\n\nNow c19: (j,m) allowed: (2,3) or (0,0). So j,m can be either (2,3) or (0,0). So two possibilities.\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: (2,0) or (3,1). So f,i either (2,0) or (3,1). Two possibilities.\n\nc17 already used.\n\nNow we have a and b fixed. Next, constraints involving a and b in component c1: we already used c1 (a,b) constraint satisfied (a=1,b=0). Next, c2 (b,c) constraint: b=0, so we need allowed (0,c) pairs from c2 allowed pairs list: allowed (b,c) pairs: [ [0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2] ]. So for b=0, c must be 3 or 2 (since (0,3) and (0,2) are allowed). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus c is either 2 or 3.\n\nNext, c3 (c,d) constraint: allowed (c,d) pairs: [ [3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1] ]. So for each possible c value we can see allowed d.\n\nIf c=2: allowed (c,d) pairs with c=2? None of the listed pairs have first element 2. So c=2 is not allowed in any (c,d) pair. So c cannot be 2. So c must be 3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3 (c=3,d) allowed pairs: from list, (3,1) is allowed. Also any other with c=3? Only (3,1). So d=1.\n\nThus d=1.\n\nNow we have a=1,b=0,c=3,d=1. So c1 assignment is fully determined: (1,0,3,1).\n\nNow we need to check that this assignment satisfies all c1 constraints: c1 (a,b) = (1,0) allowed, yes. c2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed? Yes, (0,3) is in c2 allowed pairs. c3 (c,d) = (3,1) allowed, yes. c4 (d,a) = (1,1) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (1,1). Yes. So c1 assignment is valid.\n\nThus there is exactly one possible assignment for a,b,c,d given the bridge constraints? Wait, we deduced b=0 from bridge c17. That forced a=1. Then c forced to 3, d forced to 1. So indeed only one assignment for c1 satisfies all constraints given the bridge constraints.\n\nBut we need to verify that there are no other possibilities for b from bridge c17? Bridge c17 allowed (b,e) pairs: [ [0,2], [0,3] ]. So b must be 0 indeed. So no other b.\n\nThus a is forced to 1 via c1. So c1 assignment is uniquely determined.\n\nThus the answer is a single tuple: [1,0,3,1].\n\nBut we must ensure that there exists a full solution for the rest of the variables (e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p) consistent with all constraints. Since we deduced a,b,c,d uniquely, we must check that there exists at least one extension for the rest. If not, then c1 assignment would be invalid (no full solution). But we need to check that there is at least one solution for the rest given these values.\n\nThus we need to see if we can assign e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe have fixed:\n- a=1\n- b=0\n- c=3\n- d=1\n- e ∈ {2,3} (from c17)\n- n=2 (from c20)\n- j,m ∈ {(2,3),(0,0)} (c19)\n- f,i ∈ {(2,0),(3,1)} (c18)\n- Also constraints for other variables.\n\nWe also have constraints in component c2: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e. So we need to assign e,f,g,h consistent with those constraints and bridge constraints linking e to b (already satisfied) and f to i.\n\nSimilarly c3: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i; with j,m and f,i constraints.\n\nc4: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m; with n fixed to 2, m from c19, etc.\n\nThus we need to find at least one solution for the rest.\n\nLet's attempt to find assignments.\n\nWe have variables:\n\n- e: either 2 or 3.\n- f: either 2 or 3 (depending on which branch of c18)\n- i: accordingly 0 or 1.\n- g, h: unknown.\n- j,m: either (2,3) or (0,0).\n- k,l: unknown.\n- o,p: unknown.\n\nAlso constraints:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: [ [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0] ].\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3] ].\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1] ].\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ].\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: [ [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3] ].\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0] ].\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3] ].\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ].\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed: [ [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2] ].\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed: [ [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2] ].\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ].\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0] ].\n\nAlso bridge constraints:\nc17: (b,e) = (0,2) or (0,3). Since b=0, e must be 2 or 3. So e is either 2 or 3 (consistent with earlier). So e=2 or 3.\n\nc18: (f,i) = (2,0) or (3,1). So f,i either (2,0) or (3,1).\n\nc19: (j,m) = (2,3) or (0,0). So j,m either (2,3) or (0,0).\n\nc20: (n,a) = (2,1) or (3,3). Since a=1, n=2 (as we have). So c20 satisfied.\n\nThus we have a consistent partial assignment.\n\nNow we need to see if we can find assignments for the rest.\n\nWe need to consider the two possibilities for e (2 or 3). For each, we need to assign f,i accordingly (two possibilities). And j,m accordingly (two possibilities). So total 2*2*2 = 8 combos. For each we need to see if we can assign g,h,k,l,o,p to satisfy constraints.\n\nWe need to check constraints for each component.\n\nLet's start enumerating possibilities.\n\nWe'll denote branch selections:\n\n- e ∈ {2,3}\n- (f,i) branch: either (2,0) or (3,1). Let's denote branch A: f=2,i=0; branch B: f=3,i=1.\n- (j,m) branch: either (2,3) or (0,0). Let's denote branch C: j=2,m=3; branch D: j=0,m=0.\n\nThus 8 combos.\n\nNow we need to see for each combination if there exist assignments for g,h,k,l,o,p satisfying constraints.\n\nWe need to check constraints for component c2 (e,f,g,h) and c3 (i,j,k,l) and c4 (m,n,o,p). Let's treat each component separately.\n\nComponent c2 constraints: (e,f) allowed pairs from c5, (f,g) from c6, (g,h) from c7, (h,e) from c8.\n\nGiven e and f, we need to find g,h satisfying the cycle.\n\nSimilarly c3: given i,j, find k,l satisfying constraints.\n\nc4: given m,n (n=2), find o,p satisfying constraints.\n\nThus we need to see for each branch combination if these subproblems have solutions.\n\nLet's enumerate all possibilities.\n\nWe'll treat e=2 or 3.\n\nCase enumeration:\n\n1. e=2, f=2, i=0 (branch A for f,i), j=2,m=3 (branch C), j=2,m=3.\n2. e=2, f=2, i=0, j=0,m=0 (branch D).\n3. e=2, f=3, i=1, j=2,m=3.\n4. e=2, f=3, i=1, j=0,m=0.\n\n5. e=3, f=2, i=0, j=2,m=3.\n6. e=3, f=2, i=0, j=0,m=0.\n7. e=3, f=3, i=1, j=2,m=3.\n8. e=3, f=3, i=1, j=0,m=0.\n\nNow for each, we need to check c2 constraints (e,f,g,h). Let's list allowed pairs for each relevant constraint.\n\nc5 (e,f) allowed pairs: [ [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0] ].\n\nThus allowed (e,f) pairs:\n- (2,2) allowed.\n- (2,0) allowed.\n- (3,1) allowed.\n- (1,2) allowed.\n- (1,3) allowed.\n- (1,0) allowed.\n\nThus for each combination (e,f) we need to check if it's allowed.\n\nc6 (f,g) allowed: [ [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (f,g):\n- (2,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n\nc7 (g,h) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1] ].\n\nThus allowed (g,h):\n- (2,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,1)\n\nc8 (h,e) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ].\n\nThus allowed (h,e):\n- (0,2)\n- (1,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,1)\n\nThus we need to find g,h such that (e,f) allowed, (f,g) allowed, (g,h) allowed, (h,e) allowed.\n\nSimilarly for c3 constraints.\n\nc9 (i,j) allowed: [ [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (i,j):\n- (0,2)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (2,3)\n\nc10 (j,k) allowed: [ [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0] ].\n\nThus allowed (j,k):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,1)\n- (2,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,0)\n\nc11 (k,l) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (k,l):\n- (3,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (3,3)\n\nc12 (l,i) allowed: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ].\n\nThus allowed (l,i):\n- (0,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (2,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,3)\n\nc4 constraints: c13 (m,n) allowed: [ [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2] ].\n\nGiven n=2, allowed (m,2):\n- (3,2)\n- (2,2)\n- (3,3) but second is 3, not 2, so not allowed.\n- (1,1) second 1, not allowed.\n- (0,2)\n- (1,2)\n\nThus allowed m values with n=2: m can be 3,2,0,1 (but need to match pairs exactly). So possible (m,n) pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (0,2), (1,2). So m ∈ {0,1,2,3} but only those four combos.\n\nc14 (n,o) allowed: [ [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2] ].\n\nGiven n=2, allowed (2,o):\n- (2,3)\n- (3,2) not (2,3) but (3,2) is (n=3) not applicable.\n- (1,0) not (2,?) no.\n- (1,2) not (2,?) no.\n- (0,3) not (2,?) no.\n- (2,2)\n\nThus allowed (n=2,o) pairs: (2,3) and (2,2). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc15 (o,p) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ].\n\nThus allowed (o,p) pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2).\n\nc16 (p,m) allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0] ].\n\nThus allowed (p,m) pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0).\n\nThus we need to find o,p,m satisfying c13-c16 given m from c13 and n=2.\n\nNow we need to check each of the 8 combos for existence of solutions for c2, c3, c4.\n\nWe can try to solve each case manually.\n\nFirst, we can also consider constraints linking c2 and c3 via f,i and i,j etc. But they are separate except for f,i and i,j and j,m linking.\n\nThus we can treat each component subproblem independent given the bridge assignments.\n\nThus for each combination, we need to check if there exists g,h satisfying c2 constraints, and k,l satisfying c3 constraints, and o,p,m satisfying c4 constraints.\n\nWe also need to check any cross constraints: c2 has (f,g) and (g,h) and (h,e). c3 has (i,j) and (j,k) and (k,l) and (l,i). c4 has (m,n) and (n,o) and (o,p) and (p,m). The only cross constraints are those already accounted: f,i (c18) and j,m (c19) and b,e (c17) and n,a (c20). So after fixing e,f,i,j,m, we have independent subproblems.\n\nThus we can solve each component separately.\n\nThus the problem reduces to checking existence of solutions for each component given the fixed values.\n\nThus we need to enumerate possibilities for each case.\n\nLet's start with case 1: e=2, f=2, i=0, j=2, m=3.\n\nCase 1: e=2, f=2, i=0, j=2, m=3.\n\nCheck c2 constraints:\n\n- (e,f) = (2,2). Is (2,2) allowed in c5? Yes, (2,2) is listed.\n\n- (f,g) = (2,g) must be allowed in c6. Allowed (f,g) pairs: (2,2) is allowed. Also (2,g) where g=2? That's the only with f=2. There is no (2,0) or (2,1) etc. So g must be 2.\n\nThus g=2.\n\n- (g,h) = (2,h) must be allowed in c7. Allowed (g,h) pairs: (2,0) and (2,1) are allowed. (2,2) is not listed. So h can be 0 or 1.\n\n- (h,e) = (h,2) must be allowed in c8. Allowed (h,e) pairs: (0,2), (2,3), (2,1)?? Wait c8 allowed pairs: [ [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1] ]. So allowed (h,e) = (h,2) means h=0 with e=2 is allowed (0,2). Also (h=2) with e=2? (2,2) is not listed. (h=1) with e=2? (1,2) not listed. (h=3) with e=2? (3,2) not listed. So only h=0 works.\n\nThus h must be 0.\n\nNow check (g,h) = (2,0) is allowed in c7? Yes, (2,0) is listed. So consistent.\n\nThus for case 1, we have g=2, h=0.\n\nThus c2 solved: (e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0). All constraints satisfied.\n\nNow c3 constraints: i=0, j=2.\n\n- (i,j) = (0,2) must be allowed in c9. Allowed (i,j) pairs: (0,2) is listed. Good.\n\n- (j,k) = (2,k) must be allowed in c10. Allowed (j,k) pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (2,? also (2,?)? The list includes (2,3), (2,2). Also (2,? maybe (2,? )? No other with j=2. So k can be 3 or 2.\n\n- (k,l) = (k,l) must be allowed in c11. We'll consider each possible k.\n\n- (l,i) = (l,0) must be allowed in c12. Allowed (l,i) pairs: (0,0), (2,0), (??). Let's list c12 allowed pairs: [ [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3] ]. So allowed (l,i) = (l,0) includes (0,0) and (2,0). So l can be 0 or 2.\n\nThus we need to find k,l such that (j,k) allowed, (k,l) allowed, and (l,i) allowed.\n\nWe have i=0, j=2.\n\nWe need to consider possible k values: 2 or 3.\n\nCase k=2:\n- (j,k) = (2,2) allowed? Yes, (2,2) is in c10 allowed pairs.\n- (k,l) = (2,l) must be allowed in c11. Allowed (k,l) pairs: (2,l) where l=?? Let's list c11 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3). No (2,l) pairs. So (2,l) not allowed for any l. Thus k cannot be 2. So k must be 3.\n\nCase k=3:\n- (j,k) = (2,3) allowed? Yes, (2,3) is in c10 allowed pairs.\n- (k,l) = (3,l) must be allowed in c11. Allowed (3,l) pairs: (3,0), (3,1), (3,3). So l can be 0,1,3.\n\n- (l,i) = (l,0) must be allowed in c12. Allowed (l,0) pairs: (0,0) and (2,0). So l must be 0 or 2. But from above, l ∈ {0,1,3}. Intersection gives l=0 only. So l=0.\n\nThus we have k=3, l=0.\n\nCheck (k,l) = (3,0) is allowed in c11? Yes, (3,0) is listed.\n\nCheck (l,i) = (0,0) allowed in c12? Yes, (0,0) is listed.\n\nThus c3 solved: i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0.\n\nNow c4 constraints: m=3, n=2.\n\n- (m,n) = (3,2) must be allowed in c13. Allowed (m,n) pairs: (3,2) is listed. Good.\n\n- (n,o) = (2,o) must be allowed in c14. Allowed (2,o) pairs: (2,3) and (2,2). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- (o,p) = (o,p) must be allowed in c15. Allowed (o,p) pairs: for o=2, allowed pairs? Let's list c15 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for o=2, there is no pair with first element 2. So o cannot be 2. For o=3, allowed pairs: (3,3) and (3,2). So p can be 3 or 2.\n\nThus o must be 3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow (o,p) = (3,p) must be allowed: p ∈ {3,2}.\n\n- (p,m) = (p,3) must be allowed in c16. Allowed (p,m) pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0). So (p,3) allowed only if p=3? Let's see: (3,3) is allowed. (p=0) with m=3? (0,3) is not listed. (p=1) with m=3? (1,3) not listed. (p=2) with m=3? (2,3) not listed. (p=3) with m=3? (3,3) allowed. So p must be 3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nNow check (p,m) = (3,3) allowed in c16? Yes.\n\nThus c4 solved: m=3,n=2,o=3,p=3.\n\nThus case 1 yields a full solution: all variables assigned.\n\nThus c1 assignment (1,0,3,1) is part of a solution.\n\nThus at least one solution exists.\n\nNow we need to enumerate all possible c1 assignments. We found only one possible assignment for a,b,c,d given the bridge constraints. However, we need t